```rust
pub fn with_cursor_freed<R>(&self, f: impl FnOnce() -> R) -> R {
    self.multi.set_draw_target(ProgressDrawTarget::hidden());
    let result = f();
    self.multi.set_draw_target(ProgressDrawTarget::stderr());
    result
}
```

This doesn't use `unwrap()`. It's fine.

OK wait, let me look at `TerminalUI::prompt`:

```rust
impl Prompter for TerminalUI {
    fn prompt(&mut self, prompt: &Prompt) -> Result<PromptResult> {
        #[cfg(unix)]
        claim_foreground();

        if let Some(surface) = &self.surface {
            surface.with_cursor_freed(|| prompt_user(prompt, &self.term))
        } else {
            prompt_user(prompt, &self.term)
        }
    }
}
```

So the `prompt` method conditionally calls `with_cursor_freed` to temporarily hide the progress bar while prompting the user, then returns the result directly without any unwrapping. No issues there either.

Let me reconsider where the unwrap might be hiding. Maybe it's in the dialoguer library itself when handling EOF, or perhaps I'm missing something in how these lower-level prompt functions handle edge cases.

